---
// Week 11 — the main conceptual figure: an already-existing sign system
// (left), a documented historical transmission of it (middle), and a later
// reinterpretation of the same patterns through a different mathematical
// framework (right). The point is the direction of interpretation — an
// existing notation was re-read, not invented, by the later framework — see
// docs/design-decisions.md's Register 1 Leibniz–Bouvet entry and Phase 9
// note for every date and claim below; nothing here is a new claim.
//
// Qian and Kun are the two example trigrams because they are the pair
// week 6 already names as the binary reading's documented endpoints
// (Kun = 0, Qian = 7) — not a pair chosen for this figure. Line data and
// Xiantian positions come from src/lib/trigrams.ts and
// src/lib/arrangements.ts; the binary reading comes from
// src/lib/leibnizBinary.ts. None of it is re-derived or hard-coded here.
import LineStack from "./LineStack.astro";
import { trigramInfo, type TrigramName } from "../../lib/trigrams";
import { XIANTIAN_ARRANGEMENT, positionOf } from "../../lib/arrangements";
import { leibnizBinaryDigits, leibnizBinaryValue } from "../../lib/leibnizBinary";

const EXAMPLES: TrigramName[] = ["Qian", "Kun"];
const examples = EXAMPLES.map((name) => ({
  ...trigramInfo(name),
  position: positionOf(XIANTIAN_ARRANGEMENT, name),
  digits: leibnizBinaryDigits(name),
  value: leibnizBinaryValue(name),
}));
---

<figure class="fig fig-leibniz-bouvet">
  <ol class="lb-stages">
    <li class="lb-stage">
      <h3 class="lb-stage-heading">Pre-existing notation</h3>
      <p class="lb-stage-sub">
        Yin/yang lines and trigram patterns — Shao Yong's Xiantian
        arrangement (week 6), already an existing sign system before any
        contact with Europe.
      </p>
      <ul class="lb-examples">
        {
          examples.map(({ name, glyph, lines }) => (
            <li class="lb-example">
              <LineStack lines={[...lines]} />
              <span class="lb-example-label">
                {glyph} {name}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li class="lb-connector" aria-hidden="true" />
    <li class="lb-stage">
      <h3 class="lb-stage-heading">Historical transmission</h3>
      <p class="lb-stage-sub">
        A contact stage, not an inventor step: Joachim Bouvet recognized the
        arrangement's structure and sent it — he did not create it.
      </p>
      <ol class="lb-dates">
        <li><span class="lb-date">4 Nov 1701</span> Bouvet sends Shao Yong's diagram in a letter to Leibniz.</li>
        <li><span class="lb-date">1 Apr 1703</span> The letter reaches Leibniz.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="lb-stage-sub">
        {
          examples.map(({ name, position }, i) => (
            <>
              {name} at {position}
              {i < examples.length - 1 ? ", " : " "}
            </>
          ))
        }
        in that diagram's own layout (Xiantian arrangement, week 6) — the
        positions are Bouvet's diagram's, not this figure's invention.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="lb-connector" aria-hidden="true" />
    <li class="lb-stage">
      <h3 class="lb-stage-heading">Leibniz's reinterpretation</h3>
      <p class="lb-stage-sub lb-stage-sub--prior">
        Leibniz had already drafted binary arithmetic independently, by
        1679 — before this diagram reached him.
      </p>
      <ul class="lb-examples">
        {
          examples.map(({ name, glyph, lines, digits, value }) => (
            <li class="lb-example">
              <LineStack lines={[...lines]} />
              <span class="lb-example-label">
                {glyph} {name}
              </span>
              <span class="lb-example-binary">
                later binary reading: {digits.join("")} = {value}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="lb-stage-sub">
        The same line patterns, read through his own binary-number
        framework in 1703 — a reinterpretation of an existing notation, not
        its source.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="lb-thesis">
    A structural correspondence between two notations is not evidence that
    one produced the other: <strong>structural correspondence &ne; historical
    invention</strong>.
  </p>
  <figcaption>
    Left to right is the order of interpretation, not a chain of invention —
    the pre-existing notation was transmitted, then re-read through a
    separate mathematical framework Leibniz already had.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

